d to sink the ship but it did not go  down so a haunting empty ghost ship was  found the next day and one of the most  sensational murder hunts in American  history was on detectives follow the  trail to Providence Rhode Island that's  where Hicks was found with a  hundred and twenty-one dollars and the  murdered captain's belongings he gave a  very famous colorful unbelievable  confession it's one of the strangest  documents in American history Hicks said  there were five people on the boat with  him when he committed his final crimes  the three people he killed himself and  the devil who he said possessed him and  urged him to kill the jury took seven  minutes to convict him the sentence  public execution at Bedloe's Island now  Liberty Island they wanted him to be on  high ground and they wanted him to be  able to see the water the city the  Narrows which is where the  verrazano-narrows bridge is tens of  thousands of giddy New Yorkers watched  from the harbor and the island so is  he's as he's looking out here he's  seeing thousands of people yet in the  harbor on boats yeah everywhere else  it's like a carpet of ships you can  always walk from ship to ship to ship  the ship all the way back to the city he  came onto the island surrounded by  thousands of huge howling crowd he falls  to his knees said a prayer got up and  said make haste hang me quick those were  his last words  so what was the site of the last public  execution in the city is now Liberty  Island right Statue of Liberty if you  wanted to write a book about America and  you were a novelist and you you know  thought everything wasn't always perfect  that was something you'd make up but it  happens to be true which is the fact  that right underneath at the feet of the  Statue of Liberty stood the gallows  where the last pirate was hanged it's  medieval it's medieval it was like  something out of one of those old  paintings you could come down here and  travel on this ship that Hicks was being  brought to his death and for $1 you  could travel on the same ship as them  drink all the beer you could drink all  the oysters you can eat watch the  execution this is not a story you read  about when you go to Liberty Island no  maybe it should be the government was  trying to send a warning but the  barbarism of the public execution  appalled even the most jaded tabloids it  became the last time New York City  executed someone this way what can folks  learn from the story of Albert heck  today they learn about what came before  the New York that they know they learn  about what America was like when it was  still wild and lawless and they learn  how that those elements that made that  city go are still all around us this is  still the same New York and that's what  fascinated me it was just like a picture  earlier in the stage of development it's  already all they're not sure there's a  better nonfiction writer in America than  rich Cohen he brings it every time and  this is the same history isn't always  good no is but in this case but think  about that when you go to Liberty Island  next time truth is better than fiction  yeah what about you go
 

